{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"02","chapter_title":"Productivity and Workflow Automation","title":"Calendar and Task Systems","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting calendar and task systems to privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation","cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/07/01"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_calibration"]},"content":"# Calendar and Task Systems\n\n![Calendar and Task Systems textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-02-calendar-and-task-systems.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost missed deadlines are not caused by forgetting a date. They are caused by confusing a due time with a work time. A syllabus says \"lab report due Friday 11:59 PM,\" and the student writes \"Friday\" on a list, then opens the assignment Friday at 9 PM and discovers it needs four hours. The due date was never the problem; the missing reservation of time to do the work was. This section teaches the single most important distinction in personal scheduling: the difference between a calendar (fixed-time commitments) and a task list (things with a deadline that still need a work block carved out for them).\n\nGetting this right turns a vague pile of obligations into a week you can actually execute. It is also the backbone for everything else in the chapter: templates, automation, and audits all assume you know when work happens, not just when it is due.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Separate fixed-time events (which belong on a calendar) from deadline-bound work (which belongs on a task list).\n- Schedule a work block for each task before its deadline, so the work has a reserved time and not just a due time.\n- Set reminder lead times that account for travel and setup, not just the moment the event starts.\n- Track task state (not started, in progress, done) so the list shows what is left, not just what exists.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student keeps everything in one notes app: \"read chapter 7, write lab report, email advisor, group project due.\" Every item looks equal and none has a time. Tuesday's lecture (a fixed commitment) sits next to \"write lab report\" (hours of work) as if they were the same kind of thing. The result is a list that tells you what exists but never tells you what to do next, or when.\n\nThe fix is to sort each item by one question: is this a fixed time someone else set, or is this work I must find time for? That sort is the whole skill, and it determines which tool the item belongs in.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA **calendar** holds events: things that happen at a fixed time you do not control, like a lecture, a shift, or a meeting. A **task list** holds work: things with a deadline that you must complete, where you choose when to actually do them. The classic mistake is putting a task (\"submit lab report\") on the calendar at its due time, which reserves the wrong moment, the instant it is due rather than the hours it takes to produce.\n\nThree rules make the system work:\n\n- **Events go on the calendar; tasks go on the list.** A lecture is an event. \"Write the lab report\" is a task; its deadline is a property of the task, not a calendar event.\n- **Every task earns a work block.** Before a task's deadline, you place an event on the calendar that is the time you will do it. The deadline (Friday 11:59 PM) and the work block (Wednesday 6 to 8 PM) are different entries.\n- **Reminders include lead time.** A reminder should fire early enough to act on, accounting for travel and setup, not at the start time when it is already too late.\n\nThe underlying logic is that time is a finite resource you allocate in advance. A due date is a constraint; a work block is the allocation that satisfies it.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Enter the fixed event: put the Tuesday 10:00 AM lecture on the calendar at its set time\",\"Capture the task: add Write the lab report to the task list with its Friday 11:59 PM deadline\",\"Reserve a work block: place a Wednesday 6 to 8 PM event that is the time you will actually do the task\",\"Set a reminder with lead time: have it fire before the start so travel and setup are covered, not at the deadline itself\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student puts 'submit lab report, Friday 11:59 PM' on their calendar at 11:59 PM and feels organized. What did they actually reserve?\" explanation=\"Putting the due time on the calendar reserves the instant work is due, not the hours it takes to produce. The work still has no home; it needs a separate work block.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The moment the work is due, but no time to actually do the work\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A full work block plus a reminder\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Both the deadline and the allocation\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Nothing, because tasks cannot go on a calendar\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is one week's CAT 100 load turned from a vague list into a working calendar plus task split.\n\n**Raw list (no dates, no times):**\n\n```\n- read chapter 7\n- write lab report\n- email advisor\n- group project due\n```\n\n**After splitting into calendar events and dated tasks:**\n\n```\nCALENDAR (fixed times)\n  Tue 10:00-10:50 AM   CAT100 lecture   (reminder 9:40 AM, covers 15 min walk + 5 min setup)\n  Wed 6:00-8:00 PM     Work block: lab report draft\n  Thu 4:00-5:30 PM     Work block: group project section\n\nTASK LIST (due dates + done state)\n  [ ] read chapter 7        due Tue\n  [ ] submit lab report     due Wed 11:59 PM\n  [ ] email advisor         due Thu\n  [ ] group project upload  due Fri 11:59 PM\n```\n\nThree decisions did the work:\n\n1. **Events versus tasks.** Lecture is a fixed time, so it is a calendar event. \"Submit lab report\" is work with a due date, so it is a task.\n2. **Work blocks before deadlines.** The 6 to 8 PM Wednesday block is when the lab report actually gets written; the 11:59 PM due time is only when it is handed in.\n3. **Reminder lead time.** The 9:40 AM alert leaves 20 minutes for the 15 minute walk plus 5 minute setup, instead of a useless \"class now\" ping.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor every obligation, ask in order: is it a fixed time (calendar) or work with a deadline (task)? If it is work, where is the work block that will produce it? Does its reminder leave enough lead time to act?\n\nA week is well-planned when it survives three checks:\n\n1. **Sort check:** Is every item correctly placed as either a fixed-time event or a deadline-bound task?\n2. **Allocation check:** Does every task have a work block reserved on the calendar before its deadline?\n3. **Lead-time check:** Does each reminder fire early enough to cover travel and setup, not at the start?\n\nIf a task has a deadline but no work block, it is a wish, not a plan.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe biggest misreading is \"I put the deadline on my calendar, so I am organized.\" Putting the due time on the calendar reserves the moment work is due, not the time to do it; the work still has no home. Add a separate work block.\n\nA second misreading is treating reminders as alarms that fire at the start time. A reminder at the moment class begins is useless if you are across campus. Lead time is the point of a reminder.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the system to a real conflict. You have a lecture Tuesday at 10 AM, a lab report due Wednesday at 11:59 PM, and a part-time shift Wednesday 1 to 5 PM. Sort first: lecture and shift are fixed-time events; the lab report is a task. Now allocate: the shift eats Wednesday afternoon, so the only viable work block is Wednesday 6 to 8 PM (after the shift, before the deadline). Place it on the calendar.\n\nThe walkthrough exposes a hidden risk: if you had only written \"lab due Wed,\" you would not have noticed that the shift consumed your obvious work time until Wednesday evening. By allocating the work block in advance, you discovered the squeeze on Sunday, when you could still adjust (start a day earlier or trade the shift). The system's value is catching the collision before it becomes a missed deadline.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Resolve the Tuesday-lecture, Wednesday-shift, Wednesday-deadline conflict\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Three obligations: lecture Tue 10 AM, lab report due Wed 11:59 PM, shift Wed 1 to 5 PM. Step one is to SORT. Which item is the task?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The lecture\",\"The shift\",\"The lab report\",\"All three are tasks\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Lecture and shift are fixed-time events; the lab report is work with a deadline, so it is the only task that needs a work block.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The shift eats Wednesday 1 to 5 PM. Where is the only viable work block before the deadline?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Wednesday 1 to 5 PM, during the shift\",\"Wednesday 6 to 8 PM, after the shift and before 11:59 PM\",\"Thursday morning, after the deadline\",\"No block is needed\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"After the shift, before the deadline: Wednesday 6 to 8 PM is the only window. Allocating it on Sunday surfaces the squeeze while you can still adjust.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Discovering the collision early is the whole payoff. With only ’lab due Wed’ written down, you would notice the shift ate your work time on Wednesday evening, far too late to recover.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader looks at a week and can tell instantly whether it is executable, not just populated. A calendar full of deadlines with no work blocks is a warning sign, not a plan. The reader asks where the actual hours of work will come from.\n\nA strong reader of a calendar and task system can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each deadline this week, when is the work block that will satisfy it?\n2. Which two commitments are competing for the same hours, and how is that resolved?\n3. Does each reminder give enough lead time to actually arrive or start on time?\n4. What does the task list say is left to do, as opposed to what merely exists?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is a one-week plan that separates calendar from tasks and shows a work block for each deadline. It is strong when a reader can trace every deadline to a reserved block of time and see that no two commitments silently overlap.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I moved 'submit lab report' off my calendar and made it a task with a Wednesday 11:59 PM deadline, then added a separate Wednesday 6 to 8 PM work block. I rejected scheduling the work block Friday afternoon because that left zero margin if the report ran long; an earlier block gives me a recovery day.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the system into a job with on-call hours. The fixed-event versus task split still holds, but a new constraint appears: some events are unpredictable (an on-call page can land any time). The adaptation is to leave deliberate buffer blocks rather than filling every hour, so an interruption does not topple the whole week. The core distinction transfers unchanged; what adapts is how much of the calendar you leave unreserved to absorb shocks.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the calendar and task systems packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-02-calendar-and-task-systems-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"one-week calendar and task plan with work blocks\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the one-week calendar and task plan.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the event-versus-task rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the plan.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries for CAT 100 07.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your week passes the sort, allocation, and lead-time checks: events on the calendar, tasks on the list, a work block reserved for every deadline, and reminders early enough to act.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"],"stem":"An essay is due Friday 11:59 PM. Which split correctly uses a calendar versus a task list?","options":["Both go on the calendar as all-day events","Calendar: a Thursday 6 to 8 PM 'draft essay' block; Task list: 'submit CAT100 essay' due Fri 11:59 PM","Both go in the task list with no date","Calendar: the 11:59 PM deadline only; nothing in the task list"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fixed-time work (the draft block) belongs on the calendar; the deliverable with a due date and done state belongs in the task list.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_equals_todo","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems"],"stem":"Your task list shows 'read chapter 7', 'email advisor', and 'lab report' with no dates. Why do you keep missing things?","options":["The list is too short","Tasks have no due dates, so nothing can surface or sort before it is late","The tasks should be calendar events instead","The list needs a different color theme"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Without due dates the system cannot tell you what is next. Adding a date to each task lets it sort and remind.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_no_due_date","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems"],"stem":"A project is due in 10 days and needs about 6 hours of work. You only scheduled the due date. What should you add?","options":["Nothing; the deadline is enough","Two or three work blocks across the 10 days that total about 6 hours before the due date","A second copy of the deadline","An all-day event on the due date"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The deadline is when it must be done. Scheduling work blocks ahead of it is how the 6 hours actually get spent.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_deadline_as_workblock","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_calendar_and_task_systems_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_calendar_and_task_systems"],"stem":"Class starts at 10:00 AM and you need 15 minutes to walk there plus 5 minutes to open materials. When should the reminder fire?","options":["At 10:00 AM when class starts","At 9:40 AM, about 20 minutes before, matching travel plus prep","At 11:00 AM after class","No reminder is needed for a recurring class"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Reminder lead time should cover what you must do first. A 'class now' alert gives no time to travel or prepare.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_2_no_reminder_lead","difficulty":"hard"}]}}